Answer: Method overloading is the process of defining multiple methods in the same class with: The same method name Different parameter signatures Example:

```java
public static int add(int a, int b) {
return a + b;
}
public static float add(float a, float b) {
return a + b;
}
```

Both methods are named add(), but their parameter types are different.

Answer: Methods can have the same name but accept different numbers of parameters.

```java
public static int add(int a, int b) {
return a + b;
}
public static int add(int a, int b, int c) {
return a + b + c;
}
```

The first method accepts two parameters, while the second accepts three.

Answer: Methods can have the same name and the same number of parameters when the parameter types differ.

```java
public static int add(int a, int b) {
return a + b;
}
public static float add(float a, float b) {
return a + b;
}
public static double add(double a, double b) {
return a + b;
}
```

Each method accepts two parameters, but the parameter types are different.

Answer: Methods can have the same name, the same number of parameters, and the same parameter types when their order differs.

```java
public static float add(int a, float b) {
return a + b;
}
public static float add(float a, int b) {
return a + b;
}
```

The signatures are: add(int, float) add(float, int) Therefore, both methods are valid overloads.

