Answer: ```java
class MethodOverloadingDemo {
public static int add(int x, int y) {
return x + y;
}
public static float add(float x, float y) {
return x + y;
}
public static double add(double x, double y) {
return x + y;
}
public static void main(String[] args) {
System.out.println(add(10, 20));
System.out.println(add(3.1F, 3.2F));
System.out.println(add(2.111, 3.333));
}
}
```

Output: 6.3 5.444 Line-by-line explanation add(int, int) handles integer arguments. add(float, float) handles float arguments. add(double, double) handles double arguments. add(10, 20) selects the integer method. add(3.1F, 3.2F) selects the float method. add(2.111, 3.333) selects the double method.

Answer: ```java
class OrderDemo {
public static float add(int a, float b) {
return a + b;
}
public static float add(float a, int b) {
return a + b;
}
public static void main(String[] args) {
System.out.println(add(10, 2.5F));
System.out.println(add(2.5F, 10));
}
}
```

Output: 12.5 12.5 The first call selects add(int, float), while the second selects add(float, int).

