Answer: ```java
class OverloadingRulesDemo {
// Different number of parameters
public static int add(int a, int b) {
return a + b;
}
public static int add(int a, int b, int c) {
return a + b + c;
}
// Different parameter types
public static float add(float a, float b) {
return a + b;
}
// Different parameter order
public static float add(int a, float b) {
return a + b;
}
public static float add(float a, int b) {
return a + b;
}
public static void main(String[] args) {
System.out.println(add(10, 20));
System.out.println(add(10, 20, 30));
System.out.println(add(2.5F, 3.5F));
System.out.println(add(10, 2.5F));
System.out.println(add(2.5F, 10));
}
}
```

Output: 6.0 12.5 12.5 Line-by-line explanation add(int, int) accepts two integers. add(int, int, int) differs by the number of parameters. add(float, float) differs by parameter type. add(int, float) and add(float, int) differ by parameter order. Java selects the matching method using the supplied arguments.
